Both `x` and `S` occur with multiplicity one in the reduced divisor (3.4).
Let `(0,y_p)` and `(0,y_p')` be the points over the distinct normalization
points `p,p'`.  In an etale neighborhood of `p`, the target branch supplied
by `p'` pulls back to a branch of `E` through `p`, and conversely at `p'`.
Consequently

```text
S(0,y_p)=S(0,y_p')=0,          y_p!=y_p'.               (3.6)
```

The roots in (3.6) are distinct as points, but their multiplicities in the
one-variable polynomial `S(0,y)` are the intersection multiplicities of the
immersed branches.  The producer proves immersion, not transversality, so
one must not call these roots simple.

The second polynomial distinguishes the two permitted locations and nothing
