## 4. Formanek makes `t` a primitive quartic element

Put

```text
K=C(H_1,H_2),            M=C(U),            L=C(x,y).
```

The field tower has

```text
[M:K]=4,                 [L:M]=mu.                     (4.1)
```

Because `x` is a source coordinate, Formanek's theorem gives `L=K(x)`.
Also `t-a=cx^mu`, so `x` has degree at most `mu` over `K(t)`.  Since
`K(t) subset M subset L`, the tower law gives

```text
mu*[M:K(t)]=[L:K(t)]<=mu.
```

Therefore

```text
M=K(t),                  [L:K(t)]=mu.                  (4.2)
```

Let `P_t(Z) in K[Z]` be the monic minimal polynomial of `t`.  It has degree
four.  Substitution gives a monic polynomial of degree `4mu` annihilating
`x`.  Since `[L:K]=4mu`, it is the minimal polynomial:

```text
m_x(X)=c^(-4)P_t(a+cX^mu) is irreducible over K.        (4.3)
```

Thus the cyclic symmetry is encoded in an exact irreducible quartic
composition.  This is equivalent, by the field tower, to irreducibility of
`X^mu-(t-a)/c` over `M`; the fact that its norm below is a `mu`-fold divisor
does not make the Kummer element a `p`-th power in `M`.

Set `V=A2-B`.  The restriction `pi^{-1}(V)->V` is finite etale and is
contained in `U`, so `t` is regular and integral there.  Since `O(V)` is
normal, the coefficients of `P_t` lie in `O(V)`, and

```text
O(V)[t] isomorphic to O(V)[Z]/(P_t)                    (4.4)
```

is a free rank-four order whose normalization is the finite-etale algebra
`O(pi^{-1}(V))`.

There are two useful exact consequences.

First, evaluate at the special ruling value.  A zero of `P_t(a)` on `V`
means that one sheet has `t=a`, hence lies on `Phi`; its target lies on
`C_0`.  Generically over `C_0`, there is one such sheet, the residue degree is
one, and `t-a` has valuation `mu`.  Therefore

```text
div_V(P_t(a))=mu*(C_0 intersect V).                     (4.5)
```

Because the producer makes `B` irreducible and `C_0!=B`, if `b` and `c_0`
are irreducible equations then for some `lambda in C*` and `k in Z`,

```text
P_t(a)=lambda*b^k*c_0^mu        in O(V)=C[u,v,b^(-1)]. (4.6)
```

At the generic point of `B`, the two companion valuations of `t-a` are zero
because `C_0!=B`.  The ramification prime has residue degree one.  The norm
valuation formula therefore identifies `k` with the valuation of `a-t` at
that ramification prime (with a negative value recording a pole).  The
partition `(2,1,1)` fixes its ramification index two but does not fix this
valuation.  Neither the cusp nor node packet supplies the missing integer.

The exponent `mu` in (4.6) is not bounded by the degree four in `Z`:
coefficients of `P_t` may have arbitrarily high target degree and poles along
`B`.  Thus (4.6) is a norm identity, not a degree contradiction.

Second, compare discriminants.  At every height-one prime of `V`, the
normalization algebra is etale and hence has unit discriminant.  The standard
index-discriminant identity for the two rank-four lattices in (4.4) gives

```text
div_V(Disc(P_t))=2*I_t                                 (4.7)
```

for an effective index divisor `I_t`.  A collision of two `t`-values outside
`B` makes `Disc(P_t)` vanish, so it lies on `I_t`.  This is nonnormality of
the monogenic order, not ramification of its etale normalization.

To see the local equation, pass to the completed strict henselization at an
off-`B` collision.  The quartic etale algebra splits into four copies of a
regular complete local ring.  For the two selected normalization branches of
`C_0`, choose local equations `u=0` and `v=0`.  Divisibility by `mu*Phi`
gives, after absorbing unit `mu`-th roots,

```text
t_1-a=u^mu,              t_2-a=v^mu.                   (4.8)
```

The two sheet values coincide along

```text
t_1-t_2=u^mu-v^mu=product_(zeta^mu=1)(u-zeta*v).       (4.9)
```

If the immersed branches are tangent, the factors in (4.9) need not define
distinct reduced curves; no transversality is asserted.  In the transverse
case they are `mu` distinct coincidence branches.  Along them the common
value of `t` varies, so nearby ordinary `A1` fibres contain pairs with the
same `pi`-image.  The fibres themselves remain ordinary affine lines.

At a collision on an ordinary point of `B`, the two unramified companion
sheets still give two regular functions satisfying (4.8)--(4.9).  The
rank-two ramified factor may carry a pole of `t`, so (4.4) need not extend
across `B`.  Treating `t` as integral on the ramification boundary is another
invalid shortcut.

## 5. Exact completed-local quartic controls

The following controls prove that none of the preceding phenomena is a
formal-local contradiction.  They do **not** assert that the local algebras
glue to the charged global `S_4` cover or arise from a global Keller map.

### 5.1 Collision away from `B`

Let `R=C[[u,v]]` and

```text
S=R^4,
tau=(u^mu,v^mu,1,2),
P(Z)=(Z-u^mu)(Z-v^mu)(Z-1)(Z-2).                       (5.1)
```

Then `S` is a rank-four finite-etale `R`-algebra and is the normalization of
the generically monogenic order `R[tau]`.  Near the origin,

```text
Disc(P)=unit*(u^mu-v^mu)^2.                             (5.2)
```

Take the local target curve `C_0` to have equation `uv=0`.  On the first
sheet designate `u=0` as the local branch of `Phi`; on the second designate
`v=0`.  The other branch on each sheet is residual.  Then

```text
(uv)^mu/u^mu=v^mu,       (uv)^mu/v^mu=u^mu,            (5.3)
```

which is exactly the inverse-Kummer identity on the selected sheets.  The
normalization is etale and has trivial canonical module, while the
monogenic order has precisely the square index divisor (5.2).  Thus a
same-fibre node and a nonzero conductor coexist with quartic etaleness and
`K=0`.

For a global plane-curve control of the singularity type, the polynomial map

```text
s |-> (s^2, s(s^2-1))
```

is immersive and identifies `s=1` with `s=-1`.  Its image

```text
Y^2=X(X-1)^2
```

has an ordinary node at `(1,0)` and one point at infinity.  This only shows
that an immersive polynomial normalization can have the required
self-identification; it is not an asymptotic curve of a constructed Keller
map.

### 5.2 Collision at an ordinary `(2,1,1)` point

Again let `R=C[[u,v]]`, put `w=u+v`, and take

```text
S=R[z]/(z^2-w)  times R times R.                        (5.4)
```

It is finite flat of rank four.  At `w=0` its geometric fibre has lengths
`(2,1,1)`, and the first factor is the simple ramification factor.  The two
`R` factors are the unramified companions.  On them set

```text
t_1-a=u^mu,              t_2-a=v^mu,                   (5.5)
```

and use `uv=0` for the two local branches of `C_0`.  On the ramified factor,
`t-a=z^(-1)` is a legitimate local model on the etale open obtained by
deleting `z=0`; it emphasizes that `t` need not extend over the boundary.
Equations (5.3) and (5.5) then hold on the companion sheets, while the fibre
partition is exactly `(2,1,1)`.  Both companions can therefore belong to the
same special ruling fibre without changing the quartic packet.

The companion equality locus is `u^mu-v^mu=0`.  For some parities and
relative branch positions it can contain the local branch `B`; otherwise it
merely crosses `B`.  Neither case changes the geometric fibre lengths.

### 5.3 The charged cusp and node packets

The standard cubic-discriminant algebra gives the cusp control.  For
`R=C[[s,w]]`, let

```text
S_c=R[z]/(z^3-3s*z-2w)  times R.                        (5.6)
```

Its discriminant is a nonzero scalar times `s^3-w^2`.  At the cusp origin
the fibre lengths are `(3,1)`; at a generic smooth point of the discriminant
they are `(2,1,1)`.  The hypersurface factor in (5.6) is regular because the
defining equation has nonzero derivative with respect to `w`.

For the node control, with `R=C[[u,v]]`, take

```text
S_n=R[z]/(z^2-u)  times R[w]/(w^2-v).                   (5.7)
```

The discriminant support is `uv=0`.  The origin has fibre lengths `(2,2)`;
geometrically, a generic point of either branch has `(2,1,1)`.  Both source
factors are regular.

Equations (5.4), (5.6), and (5.7) have exactly the mutually required local
packet types: transposition generically, a three-cycle at the cusp, and two
disjoint transpositions at the node.  The extra singularity belongs
to `C_0`, not to `B`, and hence does not add a cusp or node to the charged
census of `B`.  What remains unproved is global gluing with all infinity and
Keller constraints; local algebra alone cannot supply the exclusion.

## 6. Why `K_U~0` and adjunction do not close the horn

Restrict the divisor relation (2.2) to `Phi`.  Since `Phi=A1`,

```text
Pic(Phi)=0,
O_U(Phi)|Phi isomorphic to O_Phi,
O_U(E)|Phi isomorphic to O_Phi.                         (6.1)
```

Adjunction gives the same result:

```text
omega_Phi isomorphic to (omega_U tensor O_U(Phi))|Phi
             isomorphic to O_Phi.                      (6.2)
```

The section cutting out `E intersect Phi` is therefore a polynomial section
of a trivial line bundle on `A1`.  Such a section may have any finite
effective zero divisor; its balancing pole lies at the missing point at
infinity.  In particular, the at-least-two collision points do not conflict
with (6.1)--(6.2).  A projective intersection number cannot be imported
without the boundary terms of a chosen completion.

There is an analogous canonical firewall for the primitive quartic order.
The scheme

```text
X=Spec(O(V)[Z]/(P_t)) subset V times A1
```

is a hypersurface and hence Gorenstein.  Its normalization is finite etale
over `V`, so both sides have locally trivial dualizing modules.  Finite
duality identifies the relative dual fractional ideal with the normalized
dualizing module; after the displayed trivializations that fractional ideal
is principal.  This does not say that the order is normal or that its
conductor is the unit ideal.
The split model (5.1)--(5.3) realizes a nonempty square index divisor while
both normalized sheets have trivial canonical module.

Finally, `T=div_U(b o pi)` is principal and has trivial dualizing sheaf by
adjunction, exactly as in the producer.  Neither (2.4) nor (6.2) introduces
`b`, so `K_U~0` supplies no new congruence for
`N=#(T intersect Phi)` and does not select either row of (3.7).

## 7. Maximum-safe conclusion and next gate

Promote, conditional on the provisional producer, exactly the following.

> **Inverse-Kummer and primitive-quartic control.**  The residual divisor in
> `pi^*C_0=Phi+E` has class `-[Phi]`, and the regular function
> `r=(c_0 o pi)^mu/(t-a)` has divisor `mu E`.  Its Kummer extension is the
> original cyclic extension, not an independent one.  On the two polynomial
> sources this is the exact factorization (0.3)--(0.4).  Formanek's theorem
> makes `t` a primitive generator of `C(U)/C(A2)`.  The same-fibre collision
> therefore forces a monogenic index/coincidence divisor, locally
> `u^mu-v^mu=0`, but not ramification of `pi` or a bad fibre of `rho`.
> Trivial canonical class and the local quartic `(2,1,1)`, `(3,1)`, and
> `(2,2)` algebras are compatible with this configuration.

Do not promote any of the following:

1. `[E]` is independent of `[Phi]`, or `mu^2|d_1`;
2. `E` is irreducible, `r` is an `A1`-fibration, or `E` is another fibre of
   `rho`;
3. the self-identification is transverse, or the roots in (3.6) are simple;
4. the monogenic index divisor is a branch/nonproper divisor of `pi` or `H`;
5. `t` extends regularly over the ramification boundary `R`;
6. the completed-local controls glue to a global cover or a Keller map;
7. `K_U~0` makes `E intersect Phi` empty or fixes its degree.

The cheapest genuine closing moves are now sharply separated.

1. **Index-at-infinity gate.**  Prove that the forced coincidence divisor of
   the primitive quartic order contributes a positive Orevkov--Chau boundary
   correction.  Since the producer's budget is saturated, one positive unit
   would contradict the horn.  The missing theorem must distinguish index
   of `O(V)[t]` from ramification of its etale normalization.
2. **Second-fibration gate.**  Use the global pseudo-plane geometry to prove
   that a regular function with `div(r)=mu E`, where `[E]=-[Phi]` and
   `E intersect Phi` contains the collision points, has general fibre `A1`.
   Only then could uniqueness of the `A1`-ruling force a relation with `t`.
3. **Companion-resultant gate.**  Combine the norm identity (4.6) with the
   actual polynomial `q_H=b o H` to force the index divisor to meet `B` at
   the cusp or node, rather than at an allowed ordinary point or only at
   infinity.  The existing Euler identity alone does not do this.

For the charged strict-block horn, `d_1>=2` and the producer conditionally
gives `mu=d_1` after minimal-degree replacement.  The `d_1=1` pseudo-plane
row is empty already because `mu>=2` and `mu|d_1`.  This says nothing about
an arbitrary canonical rank-four normalization outside the charged
pseudo-plane package; no `d_1=1` specialization is made here.

No replay is attached.  The new statements are divisor identities, field
tower arithmetic, and exact completed-local algebra formulas valid for all
`mu`; sampling finitely many values would not verify the theorem-level
